# Doc Parser Onboarding

This feature currently works, but the ownership is split in a way that is not ideal for production.

Today:
- `doc-parser` parses documents and stores parse results
- `index-manager` creates or updates indexes from `DocumentInfo[]`
- the SDK glues those two services together

Target:
- the client should call `doc-parser` once
- `doc-parser` should own the full ingestion flow
- `doc-parser` should call `index-manager` internally
- SQS should carry the internal work
- Kafka is optional for lifecycle events, not for the main command path

## Current architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph Client
    App[App / Dashboard]
    SDK[SDK<br/>parseAndIndex / parseAndAddDocs]
    Transform[SDK<br/>transformChunksToDocuments]
  end

  subgraph ParserSide[Parser side]
    Parser[doc-parser]
    Unsiloed[Unsiloed]
    ParseStore[S3 + DynamoDB]
  end

  subgraph BuildSide[Build side]
    IndexMgr[index-manager]
    IndexStore[R2 + Supabase]
  end

  App -->|starts flow| SDK
  SDK -->|submit files / urls| Parser
  Parser -->|send parse jobs| Unsiloed
  Parser -->|store batch + results| ParseStore
  Parser -->|return parsed chunks| Transform
  Transform -->|DocumentInfo[]| IndexMgr
  IndexMgr -->|build or add docs| IndexStore
```

The main point is that the SDK currently owns the middle of the flow. It submits the parse request, waits for results, transforms chunks into `DocumentInfo[]`, and then calls `index-manager`.

## What each part does

| Part | Main responsibility | Important functions / endpoints |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `doc-parser` | Parse files or URLs and store parse job state/results | `POST /api/parse/upload`, `POST /api/parse/url`, `GET /api/parse/:jobId` |
| `index-manager` | Create or update indexes from documents | `POST /v1/manage` with `initUpload`, `startBuild`, `getJobStatus`, `addDocs`, `getIndex` |
| JS SDK | Current orchestration layer for JS users | `parse`, `getParseStatus`, `parseAndIndex`, `parseAndAddDocs` |
| Python SDK | Public Python API | `parse`, `get_parse_status`, `parse_and_index` |
| Rust `ParseClient` | Parser calls and parse-to-document collection for Python | `parse_upload`, `parse_url`, `parse_and_collect_documents_from_*` |
| Rust `ManageClient` | Index-manager calls for Python | `create_index`, `add_docs`, `get_job_status` |
| Rust `IndexManager` | Local load/query path | `load_index`, `query` |

Two clarifications matter here:

- Rust core is not a separate external service. It is the lower-level client layer used by Python.
- The Python branch reviewed here does not yet publicly expose `parse_and_add_docs(...)`, while JS already has `parseAndAddDocs(...)`.

## What we have today

The current system already supports multi-file and multi-URL parsing. `doc-parser` creates parse batches, stores job state, and returns batch or job status. `index-manager` can build a new index or add documents to an existing index. Source-file tracking and stale-index detection are also already present on the index side.

The parser side talks to Unsiloed and stores parse state in S3 and DynamoDB. The build side stores index assets and metadata in R2 and Supabase.

## What is missing today

The main missing piece is service-side orchestration. There is no `doc-parser` endpoint today that accepts one ingestion request and then internally drives parse plus index creation or add-docs.

Because of that, the SDK still owns:
- parse submission
- parse polling
- parse result collection
- chunk-to-document transformation
- the handoff into `index-manager`

There are also rollout and hardening gaps:
- Python public API still lacks `parse_and_add_docs(...)`
- parser requests are still capped at 20 files or 20 URLs
- production rollout still needs migrations, batch-shaped integration tests, and operational hardening

## Target architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Client[Client / SDK]

  subgraph ParserSvc[doc-parser]
    API[doc-parser ingestion API]
    State[ingestion job state]
    Consumer[internal SQS consumer]
  end

  SQS[SQS]
  IndexMgr[index-manager]
  Unsiloed[Unsiloed]
  Kafka[Kafka events<br/>(optional)]

  Client -->|1. submit once| API
  API -->|2. create ingestionJobId| State
  API -->|3. enqueue work| SQS
  API -->|4. return jobId| Client

  Client -->|5. poll status| API
  API --> State

  SQS --> Consumer
  Consumer -->|6. parse docs| Unsiloed
  Consumer -->|7. update progress| State
  Consumer -->|8. create index / add docs| IndexMgr
  Consumer -->|9. emit lifecycle events| Kafka
```

In the target model, `doc-parser` becomes the public owner of ingestion. The client no longer coordinates parser calls and index-manager calls. The client submits once to `doc-parser` and polls `doc-parser` for status.

The important detail is that the consumer is internal to `doc-parser`. It is not a separate public-facing service. It is just the async execution part of `doc-parser`.

## Why SQS and Kafka

SQS and Kafka should not be doing the same job.

- SQS is the command/work path: "process this ingestion job"
- Kafka is the event path: "this ingestion job moved to parsed, indexed, failed, or completed"

If the immediate need is only backend orchestration and retries, SQS is enough. Kafka becomes useful if other systems also need to subscribe to ingestion lifecycle events.

## Main public surface today

| Surface | Accepts | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST /api/parse/upload` | `multipart/form-data` with `files[]`, `projectId`, optional `indexName`, optional `options` | parse batch response with `batchId` and child jobs |
| `POST /api/parse/url` | JSON with `projectId`, `urls[]`, optional `indexName`, optional `options` | parse batch response with `batchId` and child jobs |
| `GET /api/parse/:jobId?projectId=...` | batch ID or child job ID | batch status or job status |
| `POST /v1/manage` | JSON action request | build job responses, mutation responses, or index reads |

All service calls require `projectId` and `x-project-key`. The parser accepts exactly one of `files` or `urls`, supports up to 20 files or 20 URLs per request, and currently limits upload size to 50 MB per file.

